WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.826
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.536 --> 00:00:10.870
SPEAKER: As enterprises
digitally transform themselves,

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:13.030
networks are at the foundation.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:14.890
While networks are
constantly modified

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:18.130
to support your applications,
you need a single pane of glass

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:21.520
that can address all aspects of
troubleshooting and monitoring

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.950
so you can easily diagnose
complex connectivity

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:25.690
and network performance issues.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:28.240
Network Intelligence Center
is Google Cloud's framework

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.330
for network monitoring across
the cloud and on premise.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:34.960
It improves user experience,
security, and operations

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.120
by providing a
comprehensive platform

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.060
for network monitoring,
verification, and optimization

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.370
across the cloud and Datacenter.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:45.520
Firewall Insights is a module
in network intelligence center

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.430
that helps you optimize
firewall rule configuration,

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:50.410
tighten up network
security boundaries,

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:52.990
and detect abnormal
traffic patterns.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:55.840
Large enterprises often
have thousands, sometimes

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.210
tens of thousands, of
firewall rules configured

00:00:58.210 --> 00:00:59.560
by different groups.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.390
Over time, the large
number of rules

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.700
becomes unmanageable
and introduces

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:07.140
all kinds of security risks
and operation overhead.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.520
The Firewall Insights
module helps you tackle

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.290
these challenging problems.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:12.880
I'll walk you through
a few use cases

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:15.730
where you can leverage these
new functionalities to optimize

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:18.310
your VPC firewall configuration.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.380
As firewall rules are
added by different people

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.530
at different times, new rules
can be accidentally shadowed

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:27.250
by existing rules and as a
result fail to take effect.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.770
Without automated detection,
it's very hard for you

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.440
to catch this when managing a
large number of firewall rules

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.640
configured for the same VPC.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:35.140
Suppose I

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:40.840
Have added a rule to allow
traffic from 10.1.0.0/24subnet

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:42.820
to access web app 2.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.320
When I go to VM1 in the source
subnet with IP as 10.1.0.2

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.940
and try to reach VM2 tagged
with a web app 2, it fails.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:55.960
To find out why, I'll go to the
network detail page for VM2.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.300
Here are two rules
applied to this traffic.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:04.030
The deny rule blocking traffic
from 10.1.0.0/16 range is

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:07.530
shadowing the allow rule that
allows traffic from a sub range

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:10.780
10.1.0.0/24.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.940
To avoid such incidents
in the future,

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:16.060
you want to proactively clean
up your firewall configurations

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:19.330
and automatically identify all
firewall rules accidentally

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.400
overshadowed by other rules.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.770
Firewall Insights in
Network Intelligence Center

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:26.530
provides you with a convenient
list of rules overshadowed

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:28.180
by other firewall rules.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:29.890
A rule can be
either overshadowed

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:32.680
by a single or a combination
of multiple rules.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.630
In this case, the
rule allowing traffic

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.960
on both port 8080 and
443 is overshadowed

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:42.520
by a combination of two deny
rules blocking HTTP and HTTPS

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.270
traffic respectively.

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:46.750
You can review each
shadowed rule in site detail

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.390
and adjust the rule settings to
match your application traffic

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:50.620
control needs.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:53.530
In this second scenario,
I'll show you the allow rules

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.990
with no hit in sight to
help you clean up your

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:58.240
allow rules leftover
from past projects

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.090
and tighten up your
security boundaries.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:03.970
To find out what these
unnecessary allow rules are,

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:06.520
I'll go to the Firewall Insights
in the Network Intelligence

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:07.390
Center.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:09.760
On the allow rules
with no hit card,

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:11.740
I see a list of firewall
rules that I have not

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:14.700
been hit by any traffic
in the last six weeks.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.200
We notice that there is a rule
for allowing internet traffic

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:20.110
to an app in a test
project, web app 1

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:22.600
that the project owner
forgot to clean up.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.820
The firewall insides
module automatically

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.340
detect such leftover allow
rules so that you can get

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.230
the opportunity to clean it up.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.300
Using Firewall Insights,
you can not only

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:34.270
identify problems in your
firewall rule configuration

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:37.360
but also obtain visibility
into abnormal traffic patterns

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.250
that you want to get alerted to.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:42.130
Deny rule with hits helps you
capture the traffic blocked

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.590
by the deny rules and
detect external attacks

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:47.320
or misbehaving VMs in
your cloud deployment.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.390
From the list, you
can easily drill down

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:51.160
to the corresponding
firewall logs

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.700
to review and find out where
the deny traffic came from.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.280
In summary, Firewall
Insights automatically

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.710
detects overlapping
and misconfigured rules

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:01.960
to help you maintain the
right set of firewall rules

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:03.880
that protect your
cloud deployment

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:07.000
and brings real-time visibility
to your firewall utilization

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.800
statistics.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.850
